 # Sparse Filtering (Smooth, Correlate, Morphology)
 
-Also called: sc.filters.correlate, sc.filters.maximum, scilters.minimum, sc.filters.smooth
+Also called: sc.filters.correlate, sc.filters.maximum, sc.filters.minimum, sc.filters.smooth
 
 ## Overview
-Sparse filtering applies spatial filters—such as Gaussian smoothing, correlation, and morphological max/min operations—to sparse voxel sets with associated scalar values. It solves the problem of efficiently computing neighborhood aggregations on sparse data without allocating dense memory for the entire bounding box. Unlike its sibling [Sparse Voxel Morphology (Dilate/Erode)](20127-sparse-voxel-morphology-dilate-erode.md), which modifies the geometry of the voxel set, sparse filtering computes new values for voxels while potentially expanding the support set. It mimics the behavior of `scipy.ndimage` with `mode='constant'` and `cval=0`, treating absent voxels as zero.
+Sparse filtering applies spatial filters—such as Gaussian smoothing, correlation, and morphological max/min operations—to sparse voxel sets with associated values [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently computing neighborhood aggregations on sparse data without allocating dense memory for the entire bounding box [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Sparse Voxel Morphology (Dilate/Erode)](20127-sparse-voxel-morphology-dilate-erode.md), which modifies the geometry of the voxel set, sparse filtering computes new values for voxels while potentially expanding the support set [S1, unofficial]. It mimics the behavior of `scipy.ndimage` with `mode='constant'` and `cval=0`, treating absent voxels as zero [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on a sparse voxel set represented as an $(N, 3)$ integer array of coordinates and an associated value array of length $N$. The core logic involves expanding the voxel set to cover the kernel support, computing weighted aggregations, and pruning the result.
